            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "This is a bit broad question at the moment. Mostly because \"transparency\" can mean several things:\r\n- If your primary concern is to see the text \"below\" the notification window, set [`config.window.winblend`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/2d0f10540efdbb1f5c480ebe19ce4de451936a5d/doc/mini-notify.txt#L125-L126) option to 100 (for fully transparent window).\r\n- If you primary concern is about inheriting color of window \"below\" the notification window, then setting `guibg=NONE` to relevant highlight groups is the way to go. Be mindful about the following potential issues:\r\n    - Make sure that your color scheme is loaded *before* you make changes to highlight groups. Otherwise color scheme may override them.\r\n    - Make sure to have `vim.notify()` also uses highlight groups without background (if that is where you want to have transparency). By default it uses the [following highlight groups](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/2d0f10540efdbb1f5c480ebe19ce4de451936a5d/doc/mini-notify.txt#L240-L247).\r\n\r\nWithout more detailed reproduction steps, I am afraid I can not help any further.",
